          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do European and US bodies differ on when to start medication for stage 1 hypertension?",
          "answer": "There is a difference in how European and US bodies define hypertension, which impacts when medication for stage 1 hypertension is considered.\n\nUS guidance considers a blood pressure of 130/80 mm Hg as the threshold for diagnosing hypertension. European guidance, however, sets the threshold for office hypertension at 140/90 mm Hg. The blood pressure range of 130 to 139 / 80 to 89 mm Hg, which is considered stage 1 hypertension in the US, is described as \"high-normal blood pressure\" in European guidance (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets).\n\nRegarding the initiation of medication for stage 1 hypertension, regardless of the definitional differences:\n*   Drug treatment, along with lifestyle measures, is recommended for individuals with stage 1 hypertension who also have established cardiovascular disease, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or an estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk of 10 percent or higher.\n*   For individuals with stage 1 hypertension who do not have these additional risk factors, lifestyle measures are recommended first. Drug treatment is considered after three to six months if lifestyle changes are not sufficient (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
